1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
The Hudson River, one of America's most storied and famous waterways.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Starting in the primitive beauty of the Adirondack Mountains,

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:15,000
it cuts its path through the rich soil, nurturing farms and families along the way,

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:18,000
spilling triumphantly into New York Harbor

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:22,000
before finally fading into the warm waters of the Atlantic Ocean.

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:27,000
But once, it was considered the key to the continent.

7
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:34,000
Fifty miles north of New York City, perched high above this mighty river's banks, sits

8
00:00:34,001 --> 00:00:37,000
one of the nation's oldest and most prestigious schools.

9
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:44,240
For nearly 200 years, the United States Military Academy at West Point has been training and

10
00:00:44,241 --> 00:00:48,480
instructing students in the art of war.

11
00:00:48,481 --> 00:00:52,280
Like the river above which it was built, the students of this school have come from the

12
00:00:52,281 --> 00:00:56,705
heart of the country and altered the world around them.

13
00:00:56,706 --> 00:00:59,280
West Point's purpose is to teach

14
00:00:59,820 --> 00:01:06,820
young cadets what it takes to be an officer in the U.S. Army.

15
00:01:06,821 --> 00:01:13,820
A cadet going to West Point, the academy goes to extremes to try to

16
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:27,409
to tell cadets that. This is going to be different. It's going to be hard.

17
00:01:27,410 --> 00:01:29,280
It's going to be demanding.

18
00:01:29,281 --> 00:01:36,280
If you come and if you make it, you will grow and you will have a life-shaping experience.

19
00:01:36,281 --> 00:01:42,360
The United States Military Academy is a four-year university that offers full scholarships

20
00:01:42,361 --> 00:01:43,745
to all cadets.

21
00:01:43,746 --> 00:01:51,360
The students are paid a monthly salary of nearly $600 and spend their summers

22
00:01:51,361 --> 00:01:53,960
spend their summers receiving practical military training.

23
00:01:56,060 --> 00:01:59,720
Upon arrival, new cadets undergo a basic training program,

24
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:03,760
emphasizing troop and artillery tactics.

25
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:10,100
More senior cadets spend their summers honing their leadership skills.

26
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:12,960
Roger, understood. If you make contact, let me know.

27
00:02:12,961 --> 00:02:16,760
If not, keep sweeping brakes until you hit a rock face.

28
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:19,720
When you hit that rock face, go back to the roadside of the hill

29
00:02:19,721 --> 00:02:21,020
and come down the hill, over.

30
00:02:24,420 --> 00:02:25,120
Harlan A. Harrison.

31
00:02:25,121 --> 00:02:28,980
Upon graduation, the cadets enter the regular army as second lieutenants,

32
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:31,800
where they begin their five-year service obligation.

33
00:02:32,020 --> 00:02:33,640
Joseph M. Harrison.

34
00:02:34,620 --> 00:02:36,560
Jeffrey D. Hay.

35
00:02:39,740 --> 00:02:44,560
Occupying a relatively small parcel of land on a 16,000-acre government reserve,

36
00:02:44,561 --> 00:02:51,060
25 fortress-like grey stone buildings make up the core of the school

37
00:02:51,061 --> 00:02:58,160
providing spaces for athletics, living, dining and education

38
00:02:58,161 --> 00:03:03,140
Within these walls, cadets study a broad array of subjects

39
00:03:03,141 --> 00:03:05,640
from military engineering to psychology

40
00:03:05,641 --> 00:03:09,900
but equally important are the lessons learned in leadership

41
00:03:09,901 --> 00:03:13,140
duty, honor and service to country

42
00:03:13,141 --> 00:03:21,500
We place a heavy emphasis on values development and professional socialization that goes hand

43
00:03:21,501 --> 00:03:25,460
in hand with their liberal arts baccalaureate education.

44
00:03:26,080 --> 00:03:31,320
And so we're unique in that regard, in that the professional development and the undergraduate

45
00:03:31,321 --> 00:03:34,660
education sort of go hand in glove here.

46
00:03:35,660 --> 00:03:41,480
The Corps of Cadets is comprised of over 4,000 young men and women divided in military fashion

47
00:03:41,481 --> 00:03:45,480
to regiments, battalions, and their smallest grouping,

48
00:03:45,481 --> 00:03:48,480
32 individual companies.

49
00:03:48,481 --> 00:03:52,480
The first class or fourth year cadets rank highest in the chain of command

50
00:03:52,481 --> 00:03:56,480
and preside as officers over the younger classes.

51
00:03:56,481 --> 00:03:59,480
The class system grooms the young cadets for leadership

52
00:03:59,481 --> 00:04:05,480
through a program that has been defined and refined for over 200 years.

53
00:04:05,481 --> 00:04:14,680
Cadets spend four years going through a very systematic leadership and military professional development program.

54
00:04:14,920 --> 00:04:22,280
Starting in their first year, where they are primarily followers, they are learning leadership by learning how to follow.

55
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:28,120
And gradually as the cadets gain experience into their sophomore, junior and senior year,

56
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:34,120
they take on leadership responsibilities of increasing complexity and significance.

57
00:04:35,480 --> 00:04:40,340
Through this time-tested method, leadership is not just taught, but instilled,

58
00:04:41,500 --> 00:04:45,280
reinforced by strict military discipline and regimented daily routines.

59
00:04:47,460 --> 00:04:52,120
Even the relatively simple act of feeding the Corps of Cadets is rigidly organized.

60
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:59,600
Attention all cadets! There are two minutes until assembly for lunch formation.

61
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:04,700
The uniform is, as for class, first class will wear sabers.

62
00:05:04,701 --> 00:05:07,800
This is the last minute to be called for this formation.

63
00:05:08,260 --> 00:05:09,760
Do not forget your lights.

64
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:11,340
Two minutes remain.

65
00:05:14,380 --> 00:05:16,220
Most of these cadets will succeed.

66
00:05:17,140 --> 00:05:19,360
Only 15 to 20 percent will drop out.

67
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:24,580
Those who graduate will continue at other, more advanced leadership schools

68
00:05:24,581 --> 00:05:26,740
as part of their service obligation.

69
00:05:27,720 --> 00:05:31,100
A few may even reach the highest positions of the Army's top command.

70
00:05:31,101 --> 00:05:37,600
Only 20 to 25 percent of commissioned army officers are West Point graduates.

71
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:44,920
But those who have undergone the life-altering experience share the bond of a unique and rich tradition.

72
00:05:46,360 --> 00:05:50,660
A legacy that connects some of the greatest names in the United States military

73
00:05:50,661 --> 00:05:54,500
and spans a time frame older than the nation itself.

74
00:05:54,501 --> 00:06:02,900
in the middle of the 18th century West Point was part of an almost inaccessible wilderness

75
00:06:02,901 --> 00:06:08,540
buried deep in the New York Highlands but within 25 years it would become the single

76
00:06:08,541 --> 00:06:16,440
most important piece of land on the continent during the American war for independence the

77
00:06:16,441 --> 00:06:21,560
Hudson River was the physical divide between the cities of Boston and New York and all other

78
00:06:21,561 --> 00:06:26,520
major cities and croplands to the south and west.

79
00:06:26,521 --> 00:06:30,980
Both the revolutionaries and the British recognized the extreme importance of controlling the

80
00:06:30,981 --> 00:06:31,980
waterway.

81
00:06:31,981 --> 00:06:37,620
It was essential from the beginning to be able to exchange men and material between

82
00:06:37,621 --> 00:06:39,060
the two sections.

83
00:06:39,061 --> 00:06:43,160
Indeed, both sides recognized that if the British ever gained control of the Hudson

84
00:06:43,161 --> 00:06:47,760
River Valley and prevented that exchange of men and material between the two sections,

85
00:06:47,761 --> 00:06:49,080
the revolution would be ended.

86
00:06:49,081 --> 00:06:55,320
With the outbreak of hostilities, the Americans moved quickly to keep the Hudson River from falling under British control.

87
00:06:57,120 --> 00:07:03,500
On May 25, 1775, just one month after the battles at Lexington and Concord,

88
00:07:04,240 --> 00:07:10,480
the Continental Congress issued a resolve to build a defensive position in the highlands above the Hudson River.

89
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:18,380
Located high on a bluff overlooking a very narrow bend in the river, West Point was ideal.

90
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:25,240
Enemy ships trying to sail past gun emplacements would have to navigate the twisting waterway

91
00:07:25,241 --> 00:07:29,460
while being showered by cannon fire.

92
00:07:29,461 --> 00:07:37,080
Colonel Bernard Romans and 24 crewmen arrived on August 29, 1775 to begin construction on

93
00:07:37,081 --> 00:07:42,700
the proposed fort complex.

94
00:07:42,701 --> 00:07:49,260
Five blockhouses, barracks measuring 80 by 20 feet, storehouses, and a guardroom,

95
00:07:49,740 --> 00:07:54,960
which support five batteries armed with 61 stationary and 21 swivel guns,

96
00:07:55,500 --> 00:07:58,660
as well as a fort with bastions and a magazine.

97
00:08:00,280 --> 00:08:03,160
When we think of fortifications here in the American Revolution,

98
00:08:03,161 --> 00:08:09,420
we have to think of earthen fortifications, temporary field fortifications,

99
00:08:09,740 --> 00:08:12,520
not something that you would see in Europe.

100
00:08:12,700 --> 00:08:19,700
of stone and masonry, but by and large simply built with rock and dirt.

101
00:08:19,701 --> 00:08:22,700
Unfortunately, Romans proved to be a poor engineer

102
00:08:22,701 --> 00:08:25,700
and was unable to complete the fortifications.

103
00:08:25,701 --> 00:08:30,700
He had also decided to build on what is now known as Constitution Island,

104
00:08:30,701 --> 00:08:32,700
across the river from West Point.

105
00:08:32,701 --> 00:08:35,700
Because the island had a relatively low elevation,

106
00:08:35,701 --> 00:08:38,700
the unfinished batteries would be easy targets for enemy guns.

107
00:08:38,701 --> 00:08:44,700
The fortification proved so ineffective that it was overrun by the British in the fall of 1777.

108
00:08:44,701 --> 00:08:47,700
They didn't have to fire a single shot.

109
00:08:47,701 --> 00:08:53,700
But the Americans would receive a second chance, as the British were forced to pull out and reconsolidate.

110
00:08:53,701 --> 00:08:57,700
Thanks to the work of a famous war hero named Benedict Arnold,

111
00:08:57,701 --> 00:09:02,700
who helped force the surrender of General John Burgoyne at the Battle of Saratoga.

112
00:09:02,701 --> 00:09:09,760
Capitalizing on his good fortune, General Washington ordered a brilliant Polish military

113
00:09:09,761 --> 00:09:15,940
engineer and revolutionary, Thaddeus Kosciuszko, to fortify West Point and ensure it never

114
00:09:15,941 --> 00:09:23,865
be taken again. Kosciuszko saw in West Point the future of military strongholds.

115
00:09:23,866 --> 00:09:24,566
Not a

116
00:09:24,567 --> 00:09:27,749
single fort, but a fortified position.

117
00:09:27,750 --> 00:09:31,940
Up to 2,700 soldiers would man over 100 cannons

118
00:09:31,941 --> 00:09:33,880
and additional mortars to defend the river.

119
00:09:35,240 --> 00:09:38,080
Multiple gun emplacements were built in concentric rings

120
00:09:38,081 --> 00:09:40,020
to provide fire support for each other,

121
00:09:40,480 --> 00:09:44,200
as well as protect two water-level strongholds,

122
00:09:44,340 --> 00:09:47,780
which would fire nearly point-blank on any ship

123
00:09:47,781 --> 00:09:50,880
trying to navigate the narrow and treacherous S-curve in the river.

124
00:09:52,340 --> 00:09:56,220
If you were on a British ship, sailing up the Hudson, approaching West Point,

125
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:59,460
the first thing you do is you look up,

126
00:09:59,461 --> 00:10:04,200
and you see imposing cliffs that dominate you, sheer rock walls,

127
00:10:04,780 --> 00:10:09,220
and bristling from all levels up above are artillery pieces.

128
00:10:09,980 --> 00:10:15,480
And at water's level, point blank, looking at the side of your ship, more artillery.

129
00:10:16,020 --> 00:10:21,040
So you would have been a very foolish ship captain indeed to have sailed into that.

130
00:10:21,880 --> 00:10:28,300
But the technological jewel in the crown of early West Point may have been the 1,700-foot chain,

131
00:10:28,301 --> 00:10:33,200
which was strung across the Hudson River to stop any ship that tried to pass.

132
00:10:34,380 --> 00:10:37,520
At nearly two feet long and one and a half feet wide,

133
00:10:38,340 --> 00:10:43,900
the individual links fashioned by sterling ironworks would take over three months to forge.

134
00:10:44,920 --> 00:10:48,240
Assembled at New Windsor, New York, about nine miles north of West Point,

135
00:10:48,600 --> 00:10:54,100
the chain was transported downriver and first installed on May 3, 1778.

136
00:10:54,101 --> 00:11:02,100
Floating upon 14 wooden rafts, the obstruction was positioned where the river turned sharply.

137
00:11:02,101 --> 00:11:08,100
Ships trying to safely navigate the bend would have to come to a complete stop or risk grounding,

138
00:11:08,101 --> 00:11:12,100
leaving no momentum to break through the floating barrier.

139
00:11:12,101 --> 00:11:18,100
The weight of the chain has been variously estimated at between 80 and 150 tons.

140
00:11:18,101 --> 00:11:24,100
When Fortress West Point was completed, you had the chain, you had shore batteries, forts and readouts,

141
00:11:24,101 --> 00:11:28,100
making this the strongest defensive position on the Hudson River Valley.

142
00:11:28,101 --> 00:11:32,100
West Point was nearly impregnable from the outside.

143
00:11:32,101 --> 00:11:37,100
So much so, the British never again attempted to take it by force.

144
00:11:37,101 --> 00:11:45,100
But it was vulnerable, and its weakness would make it the backdrop for America's most infamous act of treason

145
00:11:45,101 --> 00:11:49,340
and lead to the downfall of one of the nation's greatest war heroes.

146
00:11:52,340 --> 00:11:58,520
The cost to put a cadet through four years at West Point is estimated at over $225,000

147
00:11:58,521 --> 00:12:01,480
and is entirely funded by taxpayers.

148
00:12:02,800 --> 00:12:04,960
West Point will return on Modern Marvels.

149
00:12:05,580 --> 00:12:10,820
By 1779, the fortress at West Point stood as a technological triumph,

150
00:12:10,821 --> 00:12:16,540
ready to defend the strategically vital Hudson River and keep it from falling under British control.

151
00:12:17,280 --> 00:12:21,820
But cannons and fortifications would be unable to defend against West Point's greatest threat.

152
00:12:23,420 --> 00:12:27,600
Probably the most dramatic moment in the whole American Revolution,

153
00:12:28,480 --> 00:12:32,840
Washington put one of his best generals in command of West Point in 1780.

154
00:12:32,841 --> 00:12:34,180
His name was Benedict Arnold.

155
00:12:34,940 --> 00:12:40,660
But Arnold had grown very unhappy with the whole revolution and with the Continental Congress in particular.

156
00:12:40,820 --> 00:12:42,500
He felt they had been persecuting him.

157
00:12:43,160 --> 00:12:47,900
And so he tried to cut a deal with the British to surrender West Point to them.

158
00:12:48,460 --> 00:12:50,220
But all sorts of things went wrong.

159
00:12:51,540 --> 00:12:56,120
So important was the fort that the British high command dispatched their head of intelligence,

160
00:12:56,460 --> 00:12:59,300
Major John Andre, to secretly meet with Arnold.

161
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:07,100
Poor Andre was caught on his way back to New York with the final arrangements to betray the fortress

162
00:13:07,101 --> 00:13:09,060
and eventually hanged.

163
00:13:09,061 --> 00:13:12,720
and capturing Andre betrayed the whole plan,

164
00:13:13,160 --> 00:13:15,800
and West Point was saved, and the revolution, I might add.

165
00:13:17,240 --> 00:13:21,760
Benedict Arnold would flee across enemy lines and be instated as a British officer.

166
00:13:23,740 --> 00:13:26,340
And though no shells had fallen on the fortress,

167
00:13:26,980 --> 00:13:31,420
the damage done by his treason left scars deeper than cannon fire ever could.

168
00:13:33,500 --> 00:13:36,220
Additionally, Arnold's actions would do little to allay

169
00:13:36,221 --> 00:13:38,880
an already deep-rooted distrust of the military.

170
00:13:39,060 --> 00:13:42,460
amongst freedom-loving Americans.

171
00:13:42,461 --> 00:13:45,700
After the end of the revolution in 1783,

172
00:13:45,701 --> 00:13:49,060
the young nation would almost completely disband the army,

173
00:13:49,061 --> 00:13:53,660
a decision which in time would nearly destroy the country.

174
00:13:53,661 --> 00:13:56,260
In June of 1784,

175
00:13:56,261 --> 00:14:00,260
the United States of America was defended by 80 men

176
00:14:00,261 --> 00:14:03,160
and one warship.

177
00:14:03,161 --> 00:14:08,260
That was the entire defense establishment of the new United States.

178
00:14:08,261 --> 00:14:12,020
The Americans basically distrusted the army.

179
00:14:12,680 --> 00:14:19,220
They were concerned that a standing army would actually be a threat to the new republic.

180
00:14:19,221 --> 00:14:24,900
The history of nations is that in times of turmoil, of turbulence, like a revolution,

181
00:14:25,460 --> 00:14:28,200
the military all too often tends to take over.

182
00:14:28,760 --> 00:14:31,480
So their answer was to not have a military.

183
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:36,380
Instead, the United States would depend upon the militia,

184
00:14:36,381 --> 00:14:40,880
citizen soldiers who would rise up in times of need to defend the country.

185
00:14:41,880 --> 00:14:46,920
But the scarcely trained, part-time soldiers would still need to be led by trained military professionals.

186
00:14:48,480 --> 00:14:52,720
Many former revolutionaries, including the first president, George Washington,

187
00:14:53,340 --> 00:14:57,580
saw the need for a military school which would instruct officers in the art of war

188
00:14:57,581 --> 00:15:00,180
and the sciences of artillery and fortification.

189
00:15:00,181 --> 00:15:07,900
A primary concern had been the American dependence on foreign military expertise during the revolution.

190
00:15:09,940 --> 00:15:19,380
George Washington, John Adams, Alexander Hamilton, Henry Knox, Baron von Stoiben, and others suggested it would be an excellent idea to train Americans in these arts

191
00:15:19,381 --> 00:15:20,081
of war.

192
00:15:20,540 --> 00:15:25,100
When the proposal came up, however, in Washington's cabinet meeting in 1793,

193
00:15:25,620 --> 00:15:28,400
his secretary of state, one Thomas Jefferson, said,

194
00:15:28,740 --> 00:15:30,660
you can't do it, it's unconstitutional.

195
00:15:33,180 --> 00:15:35,960
Jefferson contended that nowhere in the Constitution

196
00:15:35,961 --> 00:15:38,440
was there a provision for a military academy.

197
00:15:40,620 --> 00:15:43,260
Ironically, when he assumed the presidency in 1801,

198
00:15:44,760 --> 00:15:48,800
one of his first acts would be to lobby Congress to create such a school.

199
00:15:49,780 --> 00:15:55,380
By 1802, Jefferson would succeed in pushing Congress to declare the fort at West Point,

200
00:15:56,340 --> 00:15:59,660
home to the newly created United States Military Academy.

201
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:05,160
Jefferson, who had fought this all of his life, who thought it was unconstitutional,

202
00:16:05,880 --> 00:16:13,520
but now one of his very first acts as president is to do what Washington wanted to do years and years before.

203
00:16:13,940 --> 00:16:14,640
Why?

204
00:16:14,920 --> 00:16:21,460
Well, the president swears to preserve, to protect, and to defend the Constitution.

205
00:16:22,160 --> 00:16:25,520
Now, being a man of high intellect, Jefferson understood.

206
00:16:25,920 --> 00:16:27,920
You can't do any of those without a military.

207
00:16:29,100 --> 00:16:32,680
Jefferson also realized most American colleges at the time

208
00:16:32,681 --> 00:16:35,900
were readying graduates for the church, law, or medicine.

209
00:16:36,600 --> 00:16:40,180
Not one school in the decades-old country taught engineering,

210
00:16:40,740 --> 00:16:43,460
a skill the young, underdeveloped nation desperately needed.

211
00:16:44,260 --> 00:16:48,200
To Jefferson, a small military school far from the nation's capital,

212
00:16:48,780 --> 00:16:52,420
teaching basic mathematics and simple engineering was ideal.

213
00:16:54,500 --> 00:16:58,260
Only two students would graduate West Point the first year.

214
00:16:58,940 --> 00:17:01,300
And over Jefferson's entire eight-year presidency,

215
00:17:01,800 --> 00:17:04,640
only 50 would graduate to become commissioned officers.

216
00:17:05,800 --> 00:17:10,460
There were buildings left from the Revolution, a number of them still available.

217
00:17:11,060 --> 00:17:14,080
One of those was turned into an academy.

218
00:17:14,260 --> 00:17:18,460
It was, as we are told, about the size of a small country school.

219
00:17:19,060 --> 00:17:25,140
But that was certainly large enough for the dozen or so young men who would attend there.

220
00:17:26,920 --> 00:17:29,640
The school was poorly run and underfunded,

221
00:17:30,800 --> 00:17:33,220
partially due to fear of a well-organized military,

222
00:17:34,280 --> 00:17:38,040
partially because the men charged with running the school were commissioned engineers

223
00:17:38,041 --> 00:17:43,460
and spent most of their time traveling, building and maintaining military forts.

224
00:17:44,260 --> 00:17:52,960
You reach the point, in fact, by 1812, that the superintendent and one newly arrived cadet constituted the military academy.

225
00:17:53,080 --> 00:17:54,140
It had gotten that bad.

226
00:17:55,340 --> 00:17:59,940
It would take a national tragedy for West Point to become a serious institution.

227
00:18:01,060 --> 00:18:05,700
The War of 1812 was a military disaster for the United States of America.

228
00:18:06,540 --> 00:18:09,840
The young country would see the British once again on American soil.

229
00:18:09,841 --> 00:18:14,880
The untrained militia proved no match for the regular British Army.

230
00:18:15,780 --> 00:18:23,080
Before the end of hostilities in 1814, the United States would watch in horror as its enemy marched to the nation's capital

231
00:18:23,081 --> 00:18:27,880
and set fire to the very symbol of American freedom, the White House.

232
00:18:28,720 --> 00:18:35,100
Meanwhile, the West Pointers who were in the Army performed very well, particularly in their specialty.

233
00:18:35,101 --> 00:18:40,500
Not one fort that was designed by a West Pointer in the War of 1812 was captured by the enemy.

234
00:18:41,200 --> 00:18:45,660
A number of West Pointers performed brilliantly leading troops in battle.

235
00:18:46,080 --> 00:18:53,080
So West Point came out of this war looking very good and had to be taken seriously from now on.

236
00:18:53,580 --> 00:18:59,960
In hopes of bettering the academy, in 1812, Congress passed a bill raising the entrance requirements.

237
00:18:59,961 --> 00:19:08,460
All cadets would now be between the ages of 14 and 21, and well versed in reading, writing, and arithmetic.

238
00:19:08,461 --> 00:19:19,180
While higher than before, at the time, these standards were considered inferior to other American colleges, where previous studies in Latin and Greek were expected.

239
00:19:19,181 --> 00:19:25,180
But the Academy's goal was to serve the Republic by taking young men from around the country

240
00:19:25,181 --> 00:19:28,180
who were loyal to their states and the voting public,

241
00:19:28,181 --> 00:19:31,180
and transform them into capable army officers.

242
00:19:33,180 --> 00:19:37,180
West Pointer's relative success during the War of 1812

243
00:19:37,181 --> 00:19:41,180
would also lead to additional funding from a grateful Congress.

244
00:19:41,181 --> 00:19:45,180
A few years later they built real barracks for the cadets,

245
00:19:45,181 --> 00:19:48,860
That's the first two barracks, South Barracks and North Barracks,

246
00:19:49,220 --> 00:19:55,780
were built in the middle of the second decade in the 1815 to 1817 time frame.

247
00:19:56,920 --> 00:20:00,680
But the greatest strides for the school would be made on the academic front,

248
00:20:01,160 --> 00:20:03,760
under the leadership of a former graduate and teacher,

249
00:20:05,880 --> 00:20:10,300
Captain Sylvanus Thayer, who was appointed superintendent in 1817.

250
00:20:11,540 --> 00:20:14,280
Thayer stressed daily recitation of the lessons,

251
00:20:14,281 --> 00:20:16,560
forcing students to study every night.

252
00:20:17,800 --> 00:20:20,360
Each cadet was also ranked with his peers.

253
00:20:21,300 --> 00:20:23,060
The ratings were posted every month,

254
00:20:23,400 --> 00:20:25,320
forcing competition amongst the students.

255
00:20:26,780 --> 00:20:29,480
Additionally, the best students in each subject

256
00:20:29,481 --> 00:20:30,940
were put into the same classes,

257
00:20:31,560 --> 00:20:33,900
which created an accelerated learning program.

258
00:20:35,040 --> 00:20:36,880
But perhaps Thayer's greatest contribution

259
00:20:36,881 --> 00:20:39,000
was to increase the quality of the education

260
00:20:39,001 --> 00:20:41,540
by finding better teachers.

261
00:20:42,540 --> 00:20:47,440
Luckily, the best place to find well-educated military engineers was West Point.

262
00:20:48,540 --> 00:20:53,060
And when a man graduated and did very well while he was going through West Point,

263
00:20:53,360 --> 00:20:58,120
when he graduated, Thayer would tap him to come back and teach at the school

264
00:20:58,121 --> 00:21:00,040
as soon as he could get his mitts on him.

265
00:21:00,520 --> 00:21:03,000
And sometimes they didn't even go away, he kept them.

266
00:21:03,001 --> 00:21:10,240
And so he was always looking to get the best talent for the school in every way he could think of.

267
00:21:10,241 --> 00:21:16,720
I think as individuals look over Thayer's superintendency and the fact that he was here 16 years

268
00:21:16,721 --> 00:21:22,420
and trained so many individuals who were so important in the academy as well as the nation's development

269
00:21:22,421 --> 00:21:27,940
that Thayer received the recognition as being called the father of the military academy.

270
00:21:28,940 --> 00:21:33,640
And in the minds of many, the selection of the academy's motto in 1898,

271
00:21:33,641 --> 00:21:39,900
duty, honor, country, was merely the institutionalization of Thayer's ideals into words.

272
00:21:40,240 --> 00:21:52,528
Thayer's West Point graduates would be tested in battle during frontier skirmishes with Native Americans during the 1820s and 30s, as well as the Mexican-American War

273
00:21:52,529 --> 00:21:53,229
in 1846.

274
00:21:55,520 --> 00:22:01,900
At the end of the war, Winfield Scott gave a toast at a banquet in Mexico City after the city surrendered.

275
00:22:02,380 --> 00:22:08,280
He said to West Point, but for its science, we could never have won this war.

276
00:22:08,281 --> 00:22:15,900
In 1843, Congress passed legislation requiring one cadet be admitted from each congressional

277
00:22:15,901 --> 00:22:21,240
district, making into law a tradition that had flourished for years.

278
00:22:21,241 --> 00:22:23,580
Still the classes remained small.

279
00:22:23,581 --> 00:22:28,700
That same year only 39 cadets would graduate.

280
00:22:28,701 --> 00:22:33,180
But these graduates, having completed their studies in math, French, chemistry, drawing

281
00:22:33,181 --> 00:22:40,180
and natural philosophy, today known as physics, would have a truly profound effect on America.

282
00:22:40,660 --> 00:22:44,560
West Point's greatest benefit to the country would come in the form of civil engineering

283
00:22:44,561 --> 00:22:45,261
feats.

284
00:22:45,262 --> 00:22:51,100
Many graduates were involved with the design and engineering of nationwide railroad

285
00:22:51,101 --> 00:22:58,100
and telegraph systems, as well as building harbors and lighthouses to open American waterways.

286
00:22:58,101 --> 00:23:02,240
But soon West Pointers would be called on for their skills in warfare,

287
00:23:02,241 --> 00:23:08,160
as the country they helped link with bridges, roads and rail would begin to tear itself apart.

288
00:23:09,540 --> 00:23:16,340
The first cadets to attend West Point in 1802 were paid $16 per month and received two meal rations per day.

289
00:23:17,100 --> 00:23:18,300
Modern Marvels will return.

290
00:23:20,760 --> 00:23:27,380
Described as a battle between brothers, the American Civil War would seem even more intimate for West Point graduates.

291
00:23:28,100 --> 00:23:38,759
Nearly 90% of the top military leaders on both sides, including Lee, Grant, Sherman, and even Jefferson Davis himself, had graduated from the U.S.

292
00:23:38,760 --> 00:23:40,000
Military Academy.

293
00:23:41,220 --> 00:23:48,020
Of the 60 most important battles of the Civil War, 55 were commanded on both sides by West Pointers.

294
00:23:48,940 --> 00:23:57,120
I guess the key idea that was taught to West Pointers was celerity, which is a Latin-like term for speed.

295
00:23:57,121 --> 00:24:02,300
If you wanted to get anywhere, you had to move fast if you attacked.

296
00:24:02,400 --> 00:24:06,120
It had to be done with maximum impact and maximum speed.

297
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:10,920
They also understood principles like concentration of force.

298
00:24:11,540 --> 00:24:14,740
You didn't attack somebody six different places.

299
00:24:14,860 --> 00:24:18,680
You concentrated all the men you had, and you hit them at its weakest point.

300
00:24:19,300 --> 00:24:20,680
Colleague versus colleague.

301
00:24:21,240 --> 00:24:22,540
Friend versus friend.

302
00:24:23,460 --> 00:24:24,440
Brother versus brother.

303
00:24:25,420 --> 00:24:29,700
The West Pointers would use their deadly classroom lessons against each other,

304
00:24:30,100 --> 00:24:35,020
with the horrible honor, duty, and courage expected of an academy-trained leader.

305
00:24:37,560 --> 00:24:43,360
With the end of the war in 1865, the academy would rely on new incoming cadets

306
00:24:43,361 --> 00:24:45,780
to help heal the wounds left by the conflict.

307
00:24:45,781 --> 00:24:55,120
Since 1843, the student body of the United States Military Academy at West Point has

308
00:24:55,121 --> 00:24:59,180
only allowed entrance to the school via presidential and congressional appointments.

309
00:25:00,220 --> 00:25:04,440
To lawmakers, this seemed the best way to create an officer corps that was as diverse

310
00:25:04,441 --> 00:25:07,960
as the states they represented and loyal to the voting public.

311
00:25:09,140 --> 00:25:14,300
The increase in statehood at the end of the 19th and early 20th centuries caused the student

312
00:25:14,301 --> 00:25:22,980
body to expand. By 1900, 381 students were in attendance, and additional housing and

313
00:25:22,981 --> 00:25:27,520
educational facilities needed to be built to keep pace with the burgeoning corps of cadets.

314
00:25:30,740 --> 00:25:37,580
After the turn of the century, the academy was again expanding and received money from

315
00:25:37,581 --> 00:25:40,839
Congress for the construction of new buildings.

316
00:25:40,840 --> 00:25:43,960
By this point, we had been rapidly converting

317
00:25:43,961 --> 00:25:49,960
from wood to granite and build a lot of the barracks facilities and academic buildings

318
00:25:49,961 --> 00:25:50,960
during that time.

319
00:25:50,961 --> 00:25:57,960
In 1902, Congress appropriated $6 million for the new construction.

320
00:25:57,961 --> 00:26:04,060
The renowned Boston architectural firm of Cram, Goodhue and Ferguson would win the contract

321
00:26:04,061 --> 00:26:06,960
with their stunning American Gothic design.

322
00:26:06,961 --> 00:26:14,720
They embraced what seemed to them to be the right architecture for the school.

323
00:26:14,960 --> 00:26:20,580
It required a gothic fortress look, which I think is really quite suitable for the school.

324
00:26:20,581 --> 00:26:29,600
And it gives it a warlike atmosphere, and it also gives you that feeling of order that is very important to an army.

325
00:26:30,280 --> 00:26:36,740
By 1915, five new buildings had been constructed, including the administration building.

326
00:26:36,960 --> 00:26:46,420
East Academic Building, Riding Hall, North Barracks, and one of the most enduring and identifiable landmarks to ever grace the campus, the chapel.

327
00:26:47,320 --> 00:26:53,000
Unfortunately, many of the older obsolete buildings needed to be removed to make room for the new castle-like structures.

328
00:26:55,180 --> 00:26:58,140
One of the most beloved was the old chapel.

329
00:26:58,141 --> 00:27:01,760
The alumni got rather irate.

330
00:27:02,260 --> 00:27:08,220
They'd hear this place where they had worshipped from 1836 to 1910 was going to be torn down.

331
00:27:08,680 --> 00:27:14,320
So they were able to get the structure disassembled and reestablished,

332
00:27:14,840 --> 00:27:19,420
put together brick by brick at the site of the West Point Cemetery, where it stands today.

333
00:27:22,620 --> 00:27:27,420
But the school would soon undergo an academic change to match its physical change.

334
00:27:28,140 --> 00:27:35,680
Though a victory for the United States, the Spanish-American War of 1898 would point out inadequacies in Army leadership.

335
00:27:37,520 --> 00:27:42,900
Military experts complained the tactics utilized were little advanced from the days of the Civil War.

336
00:27:43,860 --> 00:27:48,960
The Army needed to modernize its strategies and further educate its commanding officers.

337
00:27:50,200 --> 00:27:54,400
The solution? Create additional, more advanced schools.

338
00:27:54,401 --> 00:28:07,266
So West Point then fit into that array of schools by being the very first, the one that gave people their foundation of leadership, their foundation of character,

339
00:28:07,267 --> 00:28:10,760
their foundation in basic military training.

340
00:28:11,460 --> 00:28:17,820
Then, after West Point, they would go to a branch school at some point.

341
00:28:18,300 --> 00:28:23,300
Then, the Command and General Staff College at Fort Leavenworth for mid-grade officers

342
00:28:23,301 --> 00:28:29,480
would take a year or sometimes two years, and they would study, ending up with a war college.

343
00:28:29,481 --> 00:28:36,120
So, for the first time, as we begin the 20th century, you have the entire array of schools.

344
00:28:36,121 --> 00:28:42,520
The next major conflict for American forces would be on the battlefields of Europe during World War I.

345
00:28:44,720 --> 00:28:51,720
The sudden need to fill the ranks with capable officers caused West Point to graduate cadets in as little as one year.

346
00:28:52,460 --> 00:28:58,640
Though the cadets' classroom studies may have been lacking, West Point's entire purpose was to create military leaders,

347
00:28:59,380 --> 00:29:03,580
a task that involved more than studying old battles or equations in textbooks.

348
00:29:03,581 --> 00:29:10,200
Self-discipline, motivation and honorable behavior were taught by example and flourished

349
00:29:10,201 --> 00:29:15,160
in the strict military atmosphere where senior cadets would organize and assign duties to

350
00:29:15,161 --> 00:29:21,520
lower classmen, who in turn would learn by following, gaining the confidence to lead

351
00:29:21,521 --> 00:29:24,180
and look after their own subordinates.

352
00:29:24,181 --> 00:29:29,680
These lessons would serve former students well, like class of 1886 graduate John Blackjack

353
00:29:29,681 --> 00:29:32,760
Pershing, leader of the American forces in World War I.

354
00:29:34,980 --> 00:29:39,600
Pershing was the perfect example of how much West Pointers were trained to care about their men.

355
00:29:39,840 --> 00:29:43,480
When he inspected a regiment or a division, he climbed up into the halos

356
00:29:43,481 --> 00:29:49,060
to see how the men were bedded down, and he went into the kitchens and sniffed the soup

357
00:29:49,061 --> 00:29:50,828
to make sure it was edible.

358
00:29:50,829 --> 00:29:55,020
He really wanted to see his men to make sure their physical side

359
00:29:55,021 --> 00:29:58,140
of their army life was done in the best possible fashion.

360
00:29:59,680 --> 00:30:08,660
After the war, a young upstart commander and class of 1903 graduate by the name of Douglas MacArthur became superintendent at West Point.

361
00:30:10,920 --> 00:30:18,460
Having seen firsthand the rigors of modern warfare, MacArthur made physical fitness a major part of academy life.

362
00:30:20,480 --> 00:30:24,260
Gymnastics, boxing and intramural sports were added to the students' regiment.

363
00:30:24,261 --> 00:30:28,000
A good soldier was a healthy soldier.

364
00:30:28,760 --> 00:30:32,720
And the mandate, every cadet and athlete, was taken to heart.

365
00:30:34,940 --> 00:30:37,840
But with America's interests growing beyond its shores,

366
00:30:38,480 --> 00:30:41,440
the biggest changes for the United States Military Academy

367
00:30:41,441 --> 00:30:45,060
would not happen on the playing fields, but in the classrooms.

368
00:30:46,500 --> 00:30:48,480
The role of the Army had changed,

369
00:30:48,680 --> 00:30:52,020
and the role of the Academy began to change with it.

370
00:30:52,021 --> 00:30:56,154
More language was being taught. An English department was created.

371
00:30:56,155 --> 00:30:58,660
Literature was introduced in a real way.

372
00:30:59,460 --> 00:31:10,140
There was also a movement afoot, in fact, by the English professor Holt, to create a department of geography and history.

373
00:31:10,580 --> 00:31:19,180
A department that would broaden the education of the officers and give them more knowledge about parts of the world that they were going to be involved in.

374
00:31:19,181 --> 00:31:26,180
The young leaders from West Point would now be better educated than at any time in history.

375
00:31:26,181 --> 00:31:36,180
By 1930, the student body had swelled to 1,200 cadets, and West Point was feeling its growing pains.

376
00:31:36,181 --> 00:31:43,180
As the academy moves into the 1930s, the size of the academy and the number of cadets grows,

377
00:31:43,181 --> 00:31:47,180
so does the need for more facilities, more classrooms, more barracks.

378
00:31:47,181 --> 00:31:54,920
Those are added somewhat in a haphazard manner, no coordinated plan of expanding the academy.

379
00:31:54,921 --> 00:31:58,360
And that's understandable with the approach of World War II.

380
00:31:59,300 --> 00:32:04,820
The prospect of another global conflict forced the military and West Point into high gear.

381
00:32:06,500 --> 00:32:13,120
There had been a problem in the 1930s, that there was insufficient training area here for some of the military skills.

382
00:32:13,121 --> 00:32:19,780
And so in the late 1930s, early 1940s, the government purchased additional territory to the south and west

383
00:32:19,781 --> 00:32:27,920
to enable us to provide a diversified and very demanding military training program at site here at West Point.

384
00:32:28,700 --> 00:32:33,840
The additional acreage would allow cadets to experience more realistic combat training

385
00:32:33,841 --> 00:32:38,220
while providing more room for the traditional artillery and cannon practice.

386
00:32:38,221 --> 00:32:49,506
The combination of a broader education and increased practical training would help many graduates who would soon find themselves leading American troops into the

387
00:32:49,507 --> 00:32:51,960
greatest armed conflict in history.

388
00:32:54,960 --> 00:33:01,146
In 1877, Henry Ossian Flipper became the first African American to graduate from the U.S.

389
00:33:01,147 --> 00:33:03,300
Military Academy at West Point.

390
00:33:03,301 --> 00:33:10,300
World War II would embroil the United States of America in yet another global conflict.

391
00:33:10,301 --> 00:33:15,300
And the fate of the nation would rest squarely on former West Point graduates.

392
00:33:15,301 --> 00:33:20,300
Generals with names like Eisenhower and Bradley,

393
00:33:20,301 --> 00:33:26,300
Patton and MacArthur would lead the country through the most brutal conflict ever seen by humankind.

394
00:33:26,301 --> 00:33:32,300
And indeed the class of 1915 is called the class the stars fell on

395
00:33:32,301 --> 00:33:36,760
because of the 164 graduates, 59 became generals.

396
00:33:37,340 --> 00:33:42,060
Eisenhower and Omar Bradley are among the two more famous generals coming out of that class.

397
00:33:42,920 --> 00:33:44,920
But West Point's role was changing.

398
00:33:46,200 --> 00:33:47,445
Never again would U.S.

399
00:33:47,446 --> 00:33:51,520
Military Academy graduates so dominate the officer corps during wartime.

400
00:33:53,560 --> 00:33:59,340
1946 was the first year that more officers graduated from the College Reserve Officer Training Corps,

401
00:33:59,341 --> 00:34:02,400
known as ROTC, then from West Point.

402
00:34:04,260 --> 00:34:08,400
Changing world politics would continue to increase the need for educated officers.

403
00:34:10,020 --> 00:34:14,060
What we had always done in every war throughout our history

404
00:34:14,061 --> 00:34:19,120
is to build up the army when it started and at the end of it, destroy it.

405
00:34:20,360 --> 00:34:21,220
Disband it.

406
00:34:21,740 --> 00:34:26,920
And we started, we began to do that in the first year or two after World War II.

407
00:34:27,260 --> 00:34:28,920
Then the Cold War started.

408
00:34:29,340 --> 00:34:36,340
Tensions between America and their one-time ally, the Soviet Union, would forever change the political landscape.

409
00:34:36,341 --> 00:34:37,795
All branches of the U.S.

410
00:34:37,796 --> 00:34:44,340
military would stand vigilant, ready to defend American interests wherever the threat of communism appeared.

411
00:34:44,341 --> 00:34:48,340
But at West Point, changes would be slow in coming.

412
00:34:48,341 --> 00:34:55,340
In the 50s and 60s now, and here I know firsthand I was a cadet from 52 to 56.

413
00:34:55,341 --> 00:35:00,620
When I entered in 1952, we had a curriculum that was really pre-World War II.

414
00:35:01,680 --> 00:35:07,660
We picked up nuclear physics, and we had modern communications and electronics and so forth.

415
00:35:08,000 --> 00:35:16,060
But the curriculum, while within courses and within programs, there were evolutionary changes, the curriculum itself was the same.

416
00:35:16,900 --> 00:35:18,840
But that would soon change.

417
00:35:20,380 --> 00:35:23,900
Numerous all-too-real conflicts would punctuate the Cold War.

418
00:35:25,340 --> 00:35:30,560
Limited fighting in Korea and the jungles of Vietnam would create a new kind of warfare,

419
00:35:31,240 --> 00:35:33,820
and West Point would produce a new kind of officer.

420
00:35:34,820 --> 00:35:36,550
Beach head is just your initial objective.

421
00:35:36,551 --> 00:35:38,940
I think that's not a very practical way to attack a beach.

422
00:35:39,340 --> 00:35:41,940
Over the years, the art of war changed.

423
00:35:42,700 --> 00:35:47,260
An officer could no longer stand in one place, for instance, and see all of his command.

424
00:35:47,261 --> 00:35:52,500
So the demands on an officer changed from the certainty that goes with training

425
00:35:52,501 --> 00:35:55,640
to being able to deal with uncertainty.

426
00:35:56,140 --> 00:35:59,440
And the way you deal with uncertainty is you build an officer corps

427
00:35:59,441 --> 00:36:01,420
that's educated as well as trained.

428
00:36:02,000 --> 00:36:06,560
Because an educated officer is an officer who is taught

429
00:36:06,561 --> 00:36:10,800
not what to think, but how to think.

430
00:36:12,900 --> 00:36:15,720
West Point once again altered its coursework,

431
00:36:16,760 --> 00:36:18,420
creating an even broader curriculum.

432
00:36:19,420 --> 00:36:21,640
For the military, the best way to find solutions

433
00:36:21,641 --> 00:36:26,520
to complicated problems was to have leaders with extensive knowledge in many areas,

434
00:36:26,780 --> 00:36:30,180
such as politics, history, and even business.

435
00:36:31,600 --> 00:36:33,220
Business is a lot like war.

436
00:36:33,780 --> 00:36:35,700
I mean, you have an enemy, you have a competitor,

437
00:36:35,940 --> 00:36:38,080
and you deal with uncertainty, you deal with the unknown.

438
00:36:38,360 --> 00:36:39,960
And you're expected to perform.

439
00:36:40,400 --> 00:36:43,220
You've got a mission to perform to a series of standards,

440
00:36:43,221 --> 00:36:49,940
and whether it's winning in battle or whether it's building a corporation that's profitable,

441
00:36:49,941 --> 00:36:52,240
In many ways, the same skills apply.

442
00:36:53,580 --> 00:36:59,300
The 1950s and 60s would see another huge increase in the number of cadets studying at West Point.

443
00:37:00,560 --> 00:37:04,360
Over 4,400 students would push the limits of the campus

444
00:37:04,361 --> 00:37:08,480
and lead to construction of new barracks and academic buildings,

445
00:37:09,320 --> 00:37:14,220
including a mess hall capable of simultaneously feeding the entire student body.

446
00:37:15,560 --> 00:37:18,820
But acquiring funding for the expansion had been a difficult task.

447
00:37:18,821 --> 00:37:24,080
when General Westmoreland was the superintendent, Kennedy was the president,

448
00:37:24,360 --> 00:37:25,780
he came to the Army-Navy game.

449
00:37:26,320 --> 00:37:29,000
In the second half, Kennedy turns to Westmoreland and says,

450
00:37:29,200 --> 00:37:35,120
General, why is it that the Naval Academy has turned out more students for the game than has West Point?

451
00:37:35,121 --> 00:37:39,900
And, of course, anyone who knows the two schools knows that every student goes to that game.

452
00:37:40,000 --> 00:37:41,920
It wasn't a question of who turned out more.

453
00:37:42,900 --> 00:37:46,300
And General Westmoreland said, Mr. President, I'm glad you asked me that.

454
00:37:46,301 --> 00:37:55,240
So the second half of a very bad game for Army was spent with Westmoreland talking to the president about the need for more cadets.

455
00:37:55,860 --> 00:38:02,340
The increased student body and subsequent building boom had drastically changed the military academy at West Point.

456
00:38:03,460 --> 00:38:06,380
Still, many aspects of cadet life remained the same.

457
00:38:07,460 --> 00:38:09,244
Living conditions are much better than they were.

458
00:38:09,245 --> 00:38:11,320
The rooms were cool in the summer and warm in the winter.

459
00:38:11,321 --> 00:38:18,000
That certainly was not true of either season before, so that life is a little easier than it used to be.

460
00:38:18,400 --> 00:38:20,300
But there are many things that are the same.

461
00:38:21,500 --> 00:38:24,600
Everyone still wears a uniform. Everyone goes to class.

462
00:38:25,160 --> 00:38:30,920
Class sizes are small, but classes are still very much the way they were, have always been.

463
00:38:31,220 --> 00:38:40,300
So cadet life changes with the times, but there's probably more that's familiar than is unfamiliar from decade to decade.

464
00:38:41,320 --> 00:38:45,620
But the familiar comfort and traditions of West Point would offer little protection,

465
00:38:46,280 --> 00:38:46,980
as the U.S.

466
00:38:46,981 --> 00:38:51,560
military found itself mired in Vietnam and distrusted by the American public.

467
00:38:52,460 --> 00:38:56,220
At the end of the conflict, the Army and West Point were in disarray

468
00:38:56,221 --> 00:38:59,760
and would need to spend the next two decades rebuilding.

469
00:39:02,660 --> 00:39:09,380
With 18,701 pipes, the chapel organ at West Point is the world's largest church pipe organ.

470
00:39:09,381 --> 00:39:13,260
West Point will return on Modern Marvels.

471
00:39:15,560 --> 00:39:20,660
In the early 1970s, as the unpopular war in Vietnam came to an end,

472
00:39:21,780 --> 00:39:24,320
troops returning from combat would face a new enemy,

473
00:39:25,420 --> 00:39:26,560
the American people.

474
00:39:28,140 --> 00:39:32,180
The 1970s was a difficult time for the Army and for West Point.

475
00:39:32,181 --> 00:39:34,920
You had the end of the Vietnam War,

476
00:39:35,300 --> 00:39:38,140
where there was a lot of criticism of the military service

477
00:39:38,141 --> 00:39:40,300
and indeed of the military academy.

478
00:39:43,440 --> 00:39:46,520
But this hostile environment would force change

479
00:39:46,521 --> 00:39:50,500
and allow West Point to become one of the finest military schools

480
00:39:50,501 --> 00:39:51,860
the world has ever seen.

481
00:39:53,940 --> 00:39:56,840
For over 170 years at West Point,

482
00:39:57,000 --> 00:39:59,420
not one woman had been allowed admission to the school.

483
00:40:00,400 --> 00:40:03,800
A 1975 act of Congress would rectify that problem.

484
00:40:03,801 --> 00:40:10,800
In 1976, the first female cadets would enter the Army, Navy, and Air Force academies.

485
00:40:10,801 --> 00:40:16,800
With great difficulty, West Point would open its doors upon this new era.

486
00:40:16,801 --> 00:40:23,800
The first women who entered, my hat's off to them, because fewer than half were able to graduate.

487
00:40:23,801 --> 00:40:30,800
It was not an inviting place if you were a young woman entering as a plea.

488
00:40:30,801 --> 00:40:40,640
There were a hundred and some in that first class, but when they looked around at the 4,500 other cadets, they were all men.

489
00:40:41,480 --> 00:40:50,280
Despite the difficulties, in 1980, 62 women finished their senior year, becoming the first women to graduate from West Point.

490
00:40:51,040 --> 00:40:54,140
The curriculum would also change to keep pace with the times.

491
00:41:00,834 --> 00:41:11,801
The period between the 70s and the early 80s was a period of time of increasing the number of electives and beginning to see those electives being grouped in areas of

492
00:41:11,802 --> 00:41:14,840
concentration that had some coherence to them.

493
00:41:14,841 --> 00:41:16,908
We still didn't have any majors.

494
00:41:16,909 --> 00:41:24,080
It wasn't until classes in the early 80s where we saw what we would consider a traditional undergraduate major.

495
00:41:24,140 --> 00:41:29,440
Though science and engineering classes were still prerequisites for all cadets,

496
00:41:29,441 --> 00:41:33,900
by 1985 the school offered 21 majors

497
00:41:33,901 --> 00:41:38,560
in many non-military subjects, such as behavioral science

498
00:41:38,561 --> 00:41:44,080
and literature. It's important that the curriculum at West Point evolved to keep

499
00:41:44,081 --> 00:41:44,880
pace with

500
00:41:44,881 --> 00:41:48,240
changing army needs and to keep pace with the times.

501
00:41:48,241 --> 00:41:52,760
Our officers must be prepared to lead high-tech and globally engaged army.

502
00:41:54,140 --> 00:41:56,540
Duty, honor, country.

503
00:41:58,060 --> 00:42:01,040
West Point's motto encapsulates the essence of the school.

504
00:42:02,600 --> 00:42:05,280
Those who have passed through the halls of this institution

505
00:42:05,281 --> 00:42:08,640
have continually changed the world in which we live.

506
00:42:10,140 --> 00:42:13,520
So if you're interested in your complete development as a person,

507
00:42:17,520 --> 00:42:22,340
with others, this is an ideal environment to hone those skills.

508
00:42:24,140 --> 00:42:30,780
West Point's goal has always been to supply an intelligent, well-educated corps of officers to the United States Army.

509
00:42:32,300 --> 00:42:39,760
We're not educating engineers and philosophers and psychologists and mathematicians and scientists.

510
00:42:40,580 --> 00:42:49,220
We are using engineering and philosophy and psychology and mathematics and physics and chemistry to educate Army officers.

511
00:42:49,221 --> 00:42:54,340
Though the purpose has remained the same, the lessons have changed.

512
00:42:55,140 --> 00:42:59,300
Every battle or scientific achievement provides military knowledge

513
00:42:59,301 --> 00:43:02,560
which must be handed down to the next generation of leaders.

514
00:43:03,100 --> 00:43:05,380
Charles D. Hall.

515
00:43:06,680 --> 00:43:08,180
Joseph M.

516
00:43:08,260 --> 00:43:12,980
From revolution to innovation, fortress to classroom,

517
00:43:14,080 --> 00:43:16,040
West Point has grown with the country,

518
00:43:16,041 --> 00:43:19,220
producing some of the nation's greatest leaders

519
00:43:19,221 --> 00:43:22,100
by teaching the ancient art of war

520
00:43:22,101 --> 00:43:23,900
in an ever-changing world.

